=F0=9F=91=8B  Good morning. This little newsletter might be the only thing =
still tethering you to reality as we plunge into the purgatory that is the =
weeks between the holidays and the new year. To keep you grounded, we=E2=80=
=99re reminding you of the ice-cold economic impact of the country=E2=80=99=
s snow storms. How cheery =E2=80=94 enjoy.

ECONOMICS OF THE HOLIDAYS

The billion-dollar blizzard problem

If you=E2=80=99ve ever had to dig your car out from under a snow bank first=
 thing in the morning, you know that the icy stuff is not to be messed with=
.

But it can cause bigger problems than making you late for work:

- The Federal Highway Administration says the US spends $4B+ (https://thehu=
stle.co/why-snow-costs-america-a-fortune-every-year?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8T0w2x=
yXnDtpAcf1S1kdJ9B9iWCwCASQVi2PQQtDB6jY4KyqYYSgnRn0ZxiO9j8s39uMIuhyZVBLQolTR=
sD_wJqNjykQ&_hsmi=3D395297931 ) a year just clearing snow and ice.

- States can lose (https://www.highways.org/wp-content/uploads/2014/02/econ=
omic-costs-of-snowstorms.pdf?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8T0w2xyXnDtpAcf1S1kdJ9B9iWCwC=
ASQVi2PQQtDB6jY4KyqYYSgnRn0ZxiO9j8s39uMIuhyZVBLQolTRsD_wJqNjykQ&_hsmi=3D395=
297931 ) $70m=E2=80=93$700m per day in lost economic activity when facing w=
inter weather shutdowns.

Each winter, cities and states toe a delicate, icy line trying to budget ju=
st enough for unexpected weather events.

A wintry mix

Winter cleanup revolves around three things: equipment, materials, and staf=
fing.

- Snow plows are among the most important pieces of equipment, and a single=
 one can cost ~$200k.

- States spray millions of gallons of salt brine on roads each winter (Utah=
 alone used 2.1m gallons of brine and 202k tons of salt in a recent winter,=
 totaling ~$4.3m).

But when storms stack up, budgets explode:

- Boston spent $40m in 2014-15 to clear ~110 inches of snow =E2=80=94 more =
than 2x its $18m budget.

- Maryland shelled out $108m after its 2014-15 winter, which saw 67% more s=
torms.

And the costs don=E2=80=99t stop when the snow melts =E2=80=94 repairs get =
delayed, potholes multiply, and agencies raid other budgets to stay afloat.

Why cities can=E2=80=99t just =E2=80=9Cplan better=E2=80=9D

Winter budgeting is basically a weather guess.

A mild year leaves cities with unused materials and labor; a rough year for=
ces overtime, emergency contractors, and fuel spikes.

- Places built for snow, like Minneapolis (which budgets $13m), bounce back=
 quickly.

- Sunbelt cities often gamble on doing less =E2=80=94 a strategy that faile=
d spectacularly for Atlanta in 2014, when a couple inches of snow stranded =
thousands and cost $2.8m, followed by a $10.7m ice storm two weeks later.

A forecasted labor shortage

Even well-funded states are short-staffed for snow removal. A 2022 survey f=
ound 84% of agencies have higher-than-normal plow driver vacancies.

When the snow hits and the workforce isn=E2=80=99t there, the consequences =
stack up fast.

